This means in the old code, when an EOC error occurred, the function would NOT break out of the while loop. It would continue to the next iteration, where `byte_index` is still the same value. This would cause an infinite loop.

But more importantly, in the old code, after the EOC error, the function would continue to the recursive call at line 254 (the else branch). Wait, no, the EOC error is in the `if` branch, not the `else` branch. After the error message, the code would fall through to the logging code at line 263-268, then loop back to the while condition.

But the current code (in the repo) already has the fix. So the vulnerability we're looking for must be something else.

